le count: W4 total 4, I have 3, so 1 left somewhere). If I discard W4, the last W4 is still available, but no one can pung because I have 3 of them? Actually, if I discard W4, others could pung if they have a pair of W4, but since only 1 other W4 exists, they'd need two of them to pung my discard. Given only 1 left, unlikely. But possible if someone has the last W4 plus two? Actually, to pung, they need two W4 in hand plus my discard. Since only one other W4 exists, they cannot have two, so they cannot pung. So safe.  \n- Discarding T4: T4 has 4 total, 1 in my hand, 3 left unseen. Others could have a pair of T4 and pung my discard. Possible.  \n- Discarding T6: T6 total 2 left (since I have one in hand, and exposed chow used one? Actually tile count: T6: 2 left in wall. So if I discard T6, someone could pung if they have a pair of T6, but only 2 left total, so they'd need both remaining T6 to have a pair, possible but unlikely.  \n- Discarding W6: W6 total 4, I have 2, so 2 left. Others could have a pair of W6 and pung.  \n- Discarding T1: T1 total 4, I have 2, so 2 left. Others could pung if they have a pair.  \n- Discarding T8: T8 total 2 left (I have 2, and exposed chow used one? Wait: T8 total 2 left according to tile count, but I have 2 in hand, so that means all T8 are in my hand? Actually tile count says T8: 2 remaining. I have 2 in hand. So total T8 in game: 4. I have 2 in hand, exposed chow used one T8 (since chow is T6,T7,T8), so that's 3 accounted for. So one more T8 somewhere? Actually math: 4 total T8. My hand: 2. Exposed chow: 1 T8. So that's 3. So 1 left unseen. If I discard a T8, someone could pung if they have a pair of T8, but only 1 left unseen, so they cannot have a pair. So safe.  \n\nSo safest discards: W4, T8 (since only one copy left unseen, making pung impossible for others). Among these, which is more useful to keep?  \n\nKeeping W4 gives me a pung possibility (if I draw the last W4, I can declare a kong, or keep as pung). Keeping T8 gives 