g T and W suits. They already have two chows in T and one in W. They might be close to winning. Discarding a safe tile is important. B8: unshown B8:4, so many left, but is it safe? Looking at next player's history: they discarded B7, B5, B1, so they might not be collecting B suit. But they also discarded T7, T9, etc. Hard to tell.\n\nPerhaps discarding a terminal that has been discarded already is safer. Looking at history: T9 has been discarded by next player (Play T9), by me (Play T9 earlier), and by across? Not sure. T8 has been discarded by several: Play T8 by across, Play T8 by me, Play T8 by previous. So T8 might be relatively safe.\n\nBut I have T8 in hand, if I discard T8, it might be safe.\n\nGiven I want to improve hand, I'll discard the least useful tile: B8.\n\nThus I choose Play B8.\n\nReason: Discarding B8 removes an isolated tile of a suit I am not collecting, preserving flexibility to form chows or pungs within W and T suits while maintaining the possibility of \"All Simples\" by eventually discarding terminals. It also appears relatively safe based on opponents' discards.\n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
